---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const title = 'PDF to Word Free — Editable DOCX, No Signup, No Watermark';
const description =
  'Convert PDF to an editable Word document free in your browser. Extracts text and images into a genuine .docx with real heading styles. No signup, no watermark. Honest about limits: complex layouts are not preserved.';
const h1 = 'PDF to Word';
const lede =
  'Turn a PDF into an editable Word document. Free, no account, no watermark. Text and images come across; fancy layouts do not.';
---
<ToolPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  h1={h1}
  lede={lede}
  crumbs={[{ label: 'PDF tools', href: '/pdf-tools' }, { label: 'PDF to Word', href: '/pdf-to-word' }]}
  toolName="TruePDF PDF to Word"
  intro={[
    'Adobe puts PDF-to-Word export behind Acrobat Pro, a paid subscription, and even the free web version asks you to sign in to an Adobe account before it hands you the file. That is not because the conversion is expensive to run; it is because a useful feature makes a good reason to collect your email address. Reading the text out of a PDF and writing it into a Word document is a modest local computation, and it does not need your identity attached.',
    'This converter does the whole job inside your browser. It reads your PDF locally, groups the extracted text into paragraphs, detects headings by font size and gives them real Word heading styles, and embeds the document\u2019s images inline in reading order. The result is a genuine .docx file that opens in Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice, ready to edit. Nothing is uploaded anywhere, which also makes it safe for contracts and other documents you would never send to a stranger\u2019s server.',
    'Now the honest part, stated plainly: this tool extracts text and images into an editable Word document. Complex layouts, tables, and multi-column designs will not be preserved. What you get is a clean single-column document with your words, your headings, and your images, not a pixel-perfect replica of the original page. If you need every table cell and text box exactly where it was, use desktop software. If you need the words editable, this is the fast free route.',
  ]}
  steps={[
    'Drop your PDF onto the box above, or click it to browse. The file never leaves your device.',
    'Choose your options: keep "Include images" ticked to carry photos across, and tick "Read text from scanned pages" if the PDF is a scan. Then click "Convert to Word" — the PDF is read page by page in your browser.',
    'When the conversion finishes, the results list how many paragraphs, headings, and images were extracted.',
    'Download the .docx file. It opens in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice.',
    'Give the document a quick read. Headings use real Word styles, so the navigation pane and table of contents work; fix any heading the size heuristic missed.',
  ]}
  specs={[
    ['Cost', 'Free, no limits'],
    ['Signup', 'None. No account, no email.'],
    ['Output', 'A genuine .docx file, editable in Word, Google Docs, and LibreOffice'],
    ['What is preserved', 'Text content, paragraph breaks, heading structure, embedded images'],
    ['What is NOT preserved', 'Complex layouts, tables, multi-column designs, exact fonts and spacing'],
    ['Headings', 'Detected by font-size heuristics and saved as real Word heading styles'],
    ['Images', 'Embedded images are included inline; tiny decorative icons are skipped. Untick "Include images" for a text-only document.'],
    ['Scanned PDFs', 'Pages that are only images have no text to extract. Tick "Read text from scanned pages" before converting to have the text read automatically.'],
    ['Page limit', 'The first 200 pages are converted'],
    ['Where files are processed', 'In your browser, on your device'],
    ['Works offline', 'Yes, once the page has loaded'],
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Is this PDF to Word converter really free?',
      a: 'Yes. The conversion runs entirely in your browser using code that ships with this page, so there is no server cost to recover and nothing to charge for. There is no account, no daily quota, and no watermark in the Word document.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What exactly do I get in the Word document?',
      a: 'An editable .docx with your PDF\u2019s text grouped into paragraphs, headings detected by font size and saved as real Word heading styles, and the document\u2019s embedded images placed inline in reading order. What you do not get: the original layout. Tables, multi-column designs, and precise positioning are not preserved; the output is a clean single-column document.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why are tables and complex layouts not preserved?',
      a: 'A PDF does not store paragraphs or tables; it stores individual characters placed at exact positions on the page. Rebuilding tables and multi-column flow from those positions reliably is the hard part of conversion, and it is where desktop software earns its price. This tool is honest about the boundary: it extracts the content faithfully and does not pretend to rebuild the design.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is my PDF uploaded to a server?',
      a: 'No. The PDF is read locally, the text and images are extracted locally, and the Word document is assembled locally. Your file never leaves your device, which makes this suitable for contracts, financial documents, and anything else you would not upload to a free converter site.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What happens with a scanned PDF?',
      a: 'A scanned PDF is pictures of pages, not text, so there is no text layer to extract. Tick "Read text from scanned pages" before converting and the tool reads those pages with an on-device text reader, inserting the recognized words into the Word document. It is slower (the reading engine downloads the first time you use it), and handwriting or very blurry scans will come out imperfect — the results say how many pages were read this way. Without the tick, the converter still embeds any images it finds but tells you plainly how many pages had no extractable text.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How are headings detected?',
      a: 'By comparing font sizes across the whole document. Text set clearly larger than the body size becomes a heading, with three levels for large, medium, and small headings, plus short bold lines. Because it is a heuristic, always skim the result: a missed heading takes two seconds to fix with Word\u2019s styles, and the real styles make the navigation pane work.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do bold and italic survive the conversion?',
      a: 'Yes. The converter reads each word\u2019s font name, so bold and italic passages come through as real bold and italic formatting in the Word document. Underline and other effects are not detected; only bold and italic are kept.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is there a page limit?',
      a: 'The first 200 pages of a PDF are converted, which covers reports, contracts, theses, and books in the common case. Very large documents are cut off there to keep the conversion fast and memory-safe in a browser tab. If your document is longer, split it first with the split tool and convert the parts.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I convert the Word document back to PDF?',
      a: 'Yes, and you do not need a tool for it. Word, Google Docs, and LibreOffice all have a built-in Save as PDF or Print to PDF option. Edit the .docx this converter gives you, then export to PDF from the editor when the text is final.',
    },
  ]}
  usecases={[
    {
      h: 'Editing a PDF you cannot edit',
      p: 'A report, CV, or article arrives as a locked PDF and you need to change the text. Convert it to Word, edit freely, and export back to PDF when done.',
    },
    {
      h: 'Reusing content',
      p: 'Lift paragraphs, tables, and quotes from papers and reports into a new document instead of retyping them. Headings keep their levels, so the structure comes along too.',
    },
    {
      h: 'Filling forms that are not fillable',
      p: 'Convert a flat PDF form to Word, type your answers into it, then print or export. Faster than retyping the whole form from scratch.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="tool-card" slot="tool" aria-label="PDF to Word converter">
    <div id="pdf2word-dropzone" class="dropzone" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Choose a PDF file to convert">
      <div class="dz-icon"><Icon name="file" size={30} /></div>
      <div class="dz-title">Drop a PDF here</div>
      <div class="dz-sub">or click to choose a file &middot; it stays on your device</div>
    </div>
    <input type="file" id="pdf2word-input" accept="application/pdf,.pdf" hidden />
    <div id="pdf2word-error" class="error-box" hidden></div>
    <p id="pdf2word-file" class="count-label" hidden></p>
    <div class="field">
      <div class="checkline">
        <input type="checkbox" id="pdf2word-include-images" checked />
        <label for="pdf2word-include-images">Include images</label>
      </div>
      <p class="hint">Photos and figures from the PDF are placed in the Word document.</p>
      <div class="checkline">
        <input type="checkbox" id="pdf2word-ocr-scans" />
        <label for="pdf2word-ocr-scans">Read text from scanned pages</label>
      </div>
      <p class="hint">Slower. For PDFs that are photos of pages, the text is read automatically. The reading engine downloads the first time you use it, so you need to be online once.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <button id="pdf2word-convert" class="btn" disabled>Convert to Word</button>
    </div>
    <p id="pdf2word-status" class="engine-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <div id="pdf2word-result" class="result" hidden>
      <h3>Converted</h3>
      <div id="pdf2word-stats"></div>
      <button id="pdf2word-download" class="btn">Download .docx</button>
    </div>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { initPdfToWord } from '../tools/pdf2word';
    initPdfToWord();
  </script>
</ToolPage>
